  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how one cell can transfer genetic messages to another through extracellular vesicles. In the donor cell, mRNAs and small non-coding RNAs, especially miRNAs, are packaged into vesicles that form within a multivesicular body and are then released outside the cell. These vesicles move toward a neighboring acceptor cell, where two possible entry routes are suggested: direct fusion with the plasma membrane or uptake by endocytosis.\n\nAfter endocytosis, the vesicle appears to enter an endosomal compartment, and the diagram highlights another possible membrane-fusion step that would release the RNA cargo into the recipient cell’s interior. Once delivered, the transferred RNAs have different functional outcomes. mRNAs can be translated into proteins, while miRNAs act in post-transcriptional regulation of target mRNAs.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a mechanism of intercellular communication in which vesicles shuttle RNA molecules between cells, allowing donor cells to influence gene expression and protein production in recipient cells. The question marks indicate that some membrane fusion steps are proposed rather than shown as fully established.",
